      usedToTreat: "Medical induction or augmentation of labor, active management after placental delivery, prevention or treatment of postpartum hemorrhage caused by uterine atony, and selected management of incomplete or inevitable abortion under obstetric supervision.",
      description: "Oxytocin activates Gq-coupled oxytocin receptors to raise intracellular calcium and contract uterine smooth muscle; in breast myoepithelial cells, the same receptor pathway produces milk ejection. It is the synthetic form of a nine-amino-acid posterior-pituitary hormone and, in obstetric dosing, a titratable uterotonic: controlled infusion can establish coordinated labor contractions, while postpartum administration contracts the uterus around open placental-bed vessels and reduces atony-related bleeding. It is not a cervical-ripening drug, does not correct a laceration or retained placenta, and is not synonymous with all labor induction. The first nursing priorities are the indication, infusion concentration, contraction pattern, uterine resting tone, fetal response, fluid balance, and a rapid stop/escalation plan for tachysystole or fetal compromise.",
      mechanism: "Oxytocin binds oxytocin receptors on myometrial cells. These Gq/11-coupled receptors activate phospholipase C, which cleaves PIP2 into diacylglycerol and inositol trisphosphate. Inositol trisphosphate releases calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum; calcium binds calmodulin and activates myosin-light-chain kinase, allowing actin-myosin cross-bridge cycling and contraction. Oxytocin also promotes decidual prostaglandin production and gap-junction coordination. Estrogen increases receptor density near term, so uterine sensitivity rises as pregnancy advances. In mammary myoepithelial cells the same calcium-contraction pathway squeezes milk from alveoli into ducts. At vasopressin receptors, high exposure has antidiuretic activity, which explains dilutional hyponatremia and water intoxication during prolonged high-dose infusion with excessive free water.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("U.S. oxytocin labeling carries a prominent notice that it is indicated for medical rather than convenience-only elective induction. Administration requires trained obstetric personnel, continuous ability to assess uterine activity and fetal status, and immediate capability for emergency delivery. Excess stimulation can cause tachysystole, reduced uteroplacental reperfusion, fetal hypoxia, placental abruption, uterine rupture, or fetal death. Rapid IV bolus can cause marked hypotension and tachycardia. Prolonged high-dose infusion with electrolyte-free fluid can cause severe hyponatremia, seizures, coma, or maternal death."),
      adverseEffects: ["Uterine cramping, nausea, vomiting, headache, flushing, transient hypotension, tachycardia, and postpartum pain", "Tachysystole, fetal heart-rate abnormality or hypoxia, uterine rupture, placental abruption, postpartum hemorrhage from exhaustion, water intoxication, hyponatremic seizure, arrhythmia, or anaphylaxis"],
      contraindications: ["Any situation in which vaginal delivery or labor stimulation is unsafe, including significant cephalopelvic disproportion, unfavorable fetal presentation, placenta or vasa previa, prolapsed cord, fetal distress when delivery is not imminent, or uterine scar at unacceptable rupture risk.", "Do not keep titrating for a numerical contraction target when resting tone, fetal tracing, or clinical context shows inadequate uteroplacental recovery."],
      nursingEssentials: ["Use a controlled infusion pump and a clearly labeled standardized concentration. Independently verify the drug, concentration, line, dose units, and connection because oxytocin errors can produce rapid harm.", "Chart contraction frequency, duration, intensity and resting tone together with fetal heart-rate response and maternal vital signs. Stop or reduce per protocol for tachysystole and begin intrauterine resuscitation/escalation rather than waiting for the next scheduled assessment."],
      interactions: ["Dinoprostone, misoprostol, and other uterotonics can add or potentiate uterine stimulation; honor product-specific removal and waiting intervals.", "Vasopressors, QT-active anesthetics, and large free-water loads can magnify cardiovascular, rhythm, or hyponatremic risk depending on route and context."],
      keyLabs: ["No serum oxytocin level guides routine titration; monitor sodium and osmolality if prolonged high-dose infusion or water intoxication is possible, plus hemorrhage labs when treating PPH", "Contraction pattern, uterine resting tone, fetal heart-rate category, maternal blood pressure and pulse, intake/output, neurologic status, uterine tone after delivery, and quantified blood loss"],
      nclexTraps: ["More contractions are not always better. The placenta is reperfused between contractions, so tachysystole can reduce fetal oxygen delivery.", "Oxytocin treats uterine atony by contraction; persistent bleeding with a firm uterus requires evaluation for trauma, retained tissue, inversion, rupture, or coagulopathy."],
      populationRisks: populationRisks("Pediatric use is limited to specialist reproductive or endocrine contexts; this obstetric card does not provide pediatric dosing.", "Older adult use is uncommon; cardiovascular and fluid-handling reserve would increase risk if used for a specialist indication.", "Oxytocin is intentionally used during labor and postpartum under close monitoring. It crosses the placenta minimally, but fetal risk arises indirectly from excessive uterine activity and reduced placental perfusion."),
      sourceNote: "Current U.S. oxytocin injection labeling: https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=a9b62187-4141-487c-a9da-f42ad7f9b408",
