      description: "Mifepristone blocks progesterone receptors, withdrawing hormonal support from decidual tissue and sensitizing the cervix and myometrium to prostaglandins; at higher chronic exposure it also blocks glucocorticoid receptors and reduces cortisol signaling in selected patients with Cushing syndrome. In the 200-mg reproductive regimen, decidual destabilization and cervical softening prepare the uterus for misoprostol-driven contraction and evacuation. In the distinct KORLYM regimen, glucocorticoid-receptor blockade can improve cortisol-driven hyperglycemia even though measured cortisol remains high because cortisol production itself is not inhibited. The products, doses, distribution rules, interactions and monitoring are not interchangeable. Mifepristone does not treat ectopic pregnancy. After reproductive use, prolonged heavy bleeding, syncope, persistent severe pain, fever or malaise may signal hemorrhage, incomplete abortion, ectopic pregnancy or serious infection and requires a defined emergency pathway.",
      mechanism: "Mifepristone binds progesterone receptors with high affinity and prevents progesterone-dependent transcription. Decidual capillaries and tissue lose hormonal support, endogenous prostaglandin release rises, the cervix remodels, and myometrium becomes more responsive to prostaglandins. Buccal misoprostol 24 to 48 hours later activates EP receptors and produces cervical opening and uterine contraction. Mifepristone also competitively antagonizes glucocorticoid receptors. In Cushing syndrome, this blocks cortisol signaling downstream, reducing hepatic glucose output and insulin resistance despite persistent or rising serum cortisol and ACTH; therefore cortisol levels cannot be used to titrate clinical response. Excess glucocorticoid blockade can cause adrenal-insufficiency physiology without a low cortisol measurement, and progesterone blockade thickens endometrium or causes bleeding during chronic KORLYM use.",
